ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW found one REAL fail-closed hole, fixed: isFencedAssignment called deriveWorktreeLeaf(assignment.projectId, ...) before type-checking it, so a forged assignment with a non-string identity made slug()'s .replace THROW — a crash where a stable code belongs. Now typeof-guarded for projectId/attemptId, and the `path` field coerced instead of `?? null` (a non-string worktreePath would have leaked into a string-typed field). New arm asserts three forged shapes all refuse RELEASE_FENCE_MISMATCH at WORKTREE_NODE and the tree survives.
Concurrency: the API is SYNCHRONOUS, so two calls cannot interleave in-process; the real race is cross-process and its losing side is now staged directly — another process creates the tree at the derived path via raw git, and the allocator ADOPTS it (worktree count 2, parent holds exactly [leaf]), never double-creating. Release-racing-adoption is likewise unreachable in-process; cross-process it is fenced by git's own worktree lock plus the COLLISION check. TOCTOU on both existsSync gates fails closed into COMMAND_FAILED / RELEASE_UNCERTAIN.
FIXTURE BUG this exposed, worth recording: on a host with global core.autocrlf=true a tree checked out by ORDINARY git holds CRLF, while the allocator's hermetic environment neutralises only the GLOBAL config and then reads that CRLF as a modification — every adopted tree read DIRTY for a reason unrelated to the code. Fixed at the source by setting core.autocrlf=false in the temp repo's LOCAL config, which both readers honour.

